    <string name="fest_story_fiesta">Venezuela is music, beaches, arepas and friendship. This free season celebrates everyday life with a Spanish deck and good company.</string>
    <string name="fest_story_halloween">El Silbón walks the plains with his sack of bones: if his whistle sounds close, he is far; if it sounds far, he is close. La Sayona, the Chupacabras and La Llorona also roam this night.</string>
    <string name="fest_story_christmas">Hallacas, ham bread, gaitas and parrandas. In Venezuela Christmas begins with the nativity scene and cards; aguinaldos are sung from house to house until Epiphany.</string>
    <string name="fest_story_reyes">The Magi bring gifts on January 6. You write a letter, leave water and grass for the camels and share the kings cake with the family.</string>
    <string name="fest_story_valentine">Valentine is celebrated with flowers, letters and music. In the Venezuelan Andes serenades and May flowers are part of romance.</string>
    <string name="fest_story_easter">Easter celebrates the resurrection. In Venezuela families share Easter hallaca, visit churches and enjoy religious tourism together.</string>
    <string name="fest_story_semana">Processions, penitents, sacred music and reflection. In many towns flower carpets are made and the Stations of the Cross are prayed.</string>
    <string name="legend_silbon">El Silbón</string>
    <string name="legend_sayona">La Sayona</string>
    <string name="legend_chupacabra">The Chupacabras</string>
    <string name="legend_llorona">La Llorona</string>
    <string name="legend_silbon_text">A young man condemned to carry a sack of bones wanders the plains. His whistle deceives: close it sounds far, far it sounds close.</string>
    <string name="legend_sayona_text">A woman of impossible beauty appears on the roads. Whoever follows her gets lost in the brush; her laughter freezes the night.</string>
    <string name="legend_chupacabra_text">On dry nights a spiny shadow comes down from the mountains and leaves the animals without blood.</string>
    <string name="legend_llorona_text">The weeping of a mother who lost her children is heard by the rivers. Whoever hears her must not answer her call.</string>
